    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "First Regular Passenger Service\nJune 22, 1910 • Friedrichshafen, Germany\nDEU SCRAN\nRETROCOSS\nDEUTSCHLAND\n10F\nPOSTES\nREPUBLIQUE DE HAUTE-VOLTA\nDEUTSCHE BUNDESPOST\n80\n22.-6.84-1?.-\n7990\nLORICHSHAN\n2.\n:22.-6.84-12 --\n7990",
      "International Tributes to the History of Flight\nFirst Regular Passenger Service\nAlthough three of his first four lighter-than-air craft\nThe full color cachet shows one of the passenger\nwere disappointments, Count Ferdinand von Zep-\nZeppelins sailing through the skies at about 45 miles\npelin remained an optimist. In 1909, he organized\nper hour. DELAG airships were outfitted with carpets\nthe Deutsche Luftschiffarhts-Aktiengesellschaft\nand wicker armchairs. Cold lunches and wine were\n(DELAG), with the idea of starting the world's first\nserved to travelers during the flight.\npassenger airline.\nIn 1910, the LZ-7 \"Deutschland\" was flown from\nthe Zeppelin works in Friedrichshafen to Dusseldorf.\nThere, on June 22, passenger service was officially\ninaugurated with flights to Frankfurt and Baden-\nMint Stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover, showing\nthe \"Deutschland\" in flight over Friedrichshafen, was\nissued by Upper Volta in 1976. In the background is\nLake Constance (Bodensee), a popular resort area.\nBaden. Soon several more Zeppelins were added to\nthe fleet, and the service linked most of Germany's\nmajor cities. Before World War 1, DELAG had\nflown 34,228 passengers over 107,213 miles — with-\nout an injury or fatality.\nPostmark: This Cover was postmarked in Friedrichs-\nhafen, Germany on June 22, 1984, the exact anni-\nversary of the inauguration of the first passenger\nservice.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A.\nW19.006.021"
    ],
